\section*{What the Word Derivative Means}
\lessonobjective{Explain a derivative without formulas; distinguish amount, average rate, and instantaneous rate.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={Before the formulas}]
A derivative answers a very ordinary question: if the input changes a little, how does the output respond right now? Before calculus, you already met average rates such as miles per hour, dollars per item, and degrees per minute. A derivative keeps the same basic idea but focuses on one instant or one input rather than on a whole interval.

For the moment, separate three objects. The function value tells an amount, the average rate compares two amounts, and the derivative describes the limiting local rate as the two input values move together. The notation will arrive soon, but the job comes first: a derivative is a local rate of response, with units of output per unit of input.
\end{bgexposition}

\BGVTwoAConceptMap



\begin{bgexposition}[title={The word is less mysterious than the notation}]
Outside calculus, a derivative can mean something obtained from something else. That ordinary meaning is useful here: the derivative is a new quantity derived from a function. If the original function tells you an amount, the derivative tells you how quickly that amount is changing. If the original graph tells you height, the derivative tells you steepness. If the original model tells you cost, the derivative tells you how sensitive cost is to one more unit of production.

At first, keep one sentence in mind: \emph{a derivative is a local rate of response}. ``Local'' means near one input rather than across an entire interval. ``Rate'' means output change per unit of input change. ``Response'' reminds us that a derivative is not merely a slope drawn on paper; it describes how one measured quantity reacts when another changes.
\end{bgexposition}

Suppose a bicycle computer says you have traveled \(12\) miles. That is an amount. Suppose it says you covered the last \(3\) miles in \(12\) minutes. That gives an average rate. Suppose the speed display says \(18\) miles per hour right now. That is an instantaneous rate.

A derivative is the general mathematical version of the last idea. It is not limited to motion. A derivative can tell how quickly temperature changes with depth, how cost changes with production, how light intensity changes with distance, or how the area of a circle changes as its radius changes.

\begin{bgconcept}[title={Amount and rate are different questions}]
If \(C(q)\) is the total cost of producing \(q\) items, then \(C(100)\) is a dollar amount. The derivative \(C'(100)\) is a rate: approximately how many additional dollars the total cost changes for one more item near a production level of \(100\).
\end{bgconcept}

\begin{bgguided}[title={A derivative before the notation}]
A tank contains \(V(t)=100+20t-2t^2\) liters after \(t\) minutes. Compare the amount and rate at \(t=3\).
\begin{bgsolution}
The amount is found by substitution:
\[
V(3)=100+60-18=142\text{ liters}.
\]
The instantaneous rate will later be calculated as
\[
V'(t)=20-4t,
\]
so
\[
V'(3)=8\text{ liters per minute}.
\]
These numbers answer different questions. The tank contains \(142\) liters at that moment, and its contents are increasing at about \(8\) liters per minute at that moment.
\end{bgsolution}
\end{bgguided}

\begin{bgcheck}{meaning-derivative-01}{multiple_choice}{rate of change}[Quick check]
If \(T(d)\) is temperature in degrees Celsius at depth \(d\) meters, what kind of quantity is \(T'(50)\)?
\begin{bghint}Look at the output units divided by the input units.\end{bghint}
\begin{bgreveal}
It is a \boxed{\text{rate of change}} measured in degrees Celsius per meter near a depth of \(50\) meters.
\end{bgreveal}
\end{bgcheck}

