\section{The Derivative Function}
\lessonobjective{Derive a formula for \(f'(x)\) from the limit definition and interpret its graph.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={Before the formulas}]
The symbols in \emph{The Derivative as a Function} compress three different questions: what the function value is, how two values compare, and what the comparison approaches when the inputs merge. Keep those questions separate. Most confusion at the beginning of differential calculus comes from treating an instantaneous rate as if it were an ordinary quotient over zero distance or zero time. It is not. It is a limit of ordinary quotients over nonzero intervals.

As you work, translate every expression into a sentence. Identify the input, the output, the point of interest, and the units. Then decide whether the problem is asking for a number at one point, a formula for all points, or a line that represents local behavior. This slower reading habit quickly becomes faster than trying to repair symbol errors after several lines of algebra.
\end{bgexposition}



Computing \(f'(a)\) at one point answers one local question. Repeating the process for every allowable input creates the derivative function \(f'\). Its graph is a compact record of all the slopes of \(f\): height on the \(f'\)-graph corresponds to slope on the \(f\)-graph.

This translation is one of the most important habits in calculus. When \(f'\) is positive, \(f\) rises; when \(f'=0\), \(f\) has a horizontal tangent; and when \(|f'|\) is large, \(f\) changes rapidly. Later chapters will extract an astonishing amount of global information from this local slope function.

A derivative at one point is a number. If we repeat the calculation for every input where the derivative exists, the resulting values form a new function.

\begin{bgdefinition}[title={Derivative function}]
The derivative function of \(f\) is
\[
\boxed{f'(x)=\lim_{h\to0}\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}},
\]
wherever this limit exists.
\end{bgdefinition}

\begin{bgguided}[title={Build the derivative of \(x^2\)}]
Find a formula for the derivative of \(f(x)=x^2\).
\begin{bgsolution}
Use a general input \(x\), not a particular number:
\[
\begin{aligned}
f'(x)
&=\lim_{h\to0}\frac{(x+h)^2-x^2}{h}\\
&=\lim_{h\to0}\frac{x^2+2xh+h^2-x^2}{h}\\
&=\lim_{h\to0}\frac{2xh+h^2}{h}\\
&=\lim_{h\to0}(2x+h)\\
&=\boxed{2x}.
\end{aligned}
\]
The original function returns a height \(x^2\). The derivative function returns the tangent slope \(2x\) at each input.
\end{bgsolution}
\end{bgguided}

\begin{bgconcept}[title={Heights on \(f'\) are slopes on \(f\)}]
If \(f'(2)=4\), then the point \((2,4)\) lies on the graph of \(f'\), and the tangent to \(f\) at input \(2\) has slope \(4\). A derivative graph is a slope record of the original graph.
\end{bgconcept}

