\item Use the limit definition to find the derivative function of \(f(x)=3x+5\).
\item Use the limit definition to find the derivative function of \(f(x)=x^2-6x\).
\item If a graph of \(f\) is increasing and becoming steeper, what signs should \(f'\) and its trend have?
\item Sketch a possible graph of \(f'\) when \(f\) is a line with slope \(-3\).
\end{bgexercises}


\begin{bgapplication}[title={Fuel use and speed sensitivity}]
Suppose a vehicle's fuel use over a fixed route is modeled by
\[
F(v)=4+\frac{(v-55)^2}{900}
\]
gallons when the average speed is \(v\) miles per hour. Then
\[
F'(v)=\frac{v-55}{450}.
\]
At \(v=70\), \(F'(70)=1/30\) gallon per mph. Near \(70\) mph, increasing speed by one mph raises predicted fuel use by about \(0.033\) gallon. At \(v=55\), the derivative is zero, indicating the locally most fuel-efficient speed in this model.
\end{bgapplication}

\begin{bgadvanced}[title={Advanced note: derivative functions can be surprisingly irregular}]
A derivative need not be continuous. For example, a carefully chosen function can be differentiable at every point while its derivative oscillates wildly near one point. Yet derivatives are not completely arbitrary: they satisfy the intermediate value property known as Darboux's Theorem. A derivative cannot jump directly from one value to another without taking every value in between, even when the derivative itself is discontinuous.
\end{bgadvanced}
