\section{When a Derivative Exists}
\lessonobjective{Explain why differentiability implies continuity and identify common ways differentiability fails.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={Before the formulas}]
The main idea behind \emph{Differentiability and Continuity} is local change. A graph may be complicated over a large interval and still behave in a simple, nearly linear way near one input. The derivative records that local direction. It does not describe the total amount of the function, and it does not automatically describe what happens far from the point.

Use three representations whenever possible: a numerical rate from nearby values, a slope on a graph, and a symbolic limit or derivative. When all three tell the same story, the calculation is much easier to trust. When they disagree, the disagreement usually exposes a dropped sign, a misread unit, or a function that is not differentiable at the point.
\end{bgexposition}

\BGVTwoADifferentiabilityGallery



A derivative exists only when nearby secant slopes approach one finite value. A jump breaks the underlying function values; a corner gives conflicting one-sided slopes; a cusp sends the slopes toward opposite infinities; and a vertical tangent may produce an infinite slope rather than an ordinary derivative.

Differentiability is stricter than continuity. A differentiable function must be continuous, but a continuous graph may still turn too sharply to have a derivative. The absolute-value graph is the standard reminder: it has no hole or jump at the origin, yet its left and right slopes disagree.

\begin{bgtheorem}[title={Differentiability implies continuity}]
If \(f\) is differentiable at \(x=a\), then \(f\) is continuous at \(a\).
\end{bgtheorem}

\begin{bgproofidea}
Write
\[
f(x)-f(a)=\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a}(x-a).
\]
As \(x\to a\), the quotient approaches \(f'(a)\) and \(x-a\to0\). Their product approaches \(0\), so \(f(x)\to f(a)\). A tangent slope cannot exist at a point where the graph is torn apart.
\end{bgproofidea}

The converse is false. A continuous function may fail to be differentiable.

Common failure modes include:
\begin{description}
\item[Discontinuity.] A jump, hole, or vertical asymptote prevents differentiability.
\item[Corner.] Left and right derivatives are finite but unequal, as with \(|x|\) at \(0\).
\item[Cusp.] One-sided slopes become oppositely infinite, as with \(x^{2/3}\) at \(0\).
\item[Vertical tangent.] Slopes become infinite with the same sign, as with \(x^{1/3}\) at \(0\).
\item[Oscillation.] Tangent behavior does not settle.
\end{description}

\begin{bgguided}[title={Why \(|x|\) is not differentiable at zero}]
Let \(f(x)=|x|\). Compute the one-sided derivative quotients at \(0\).
\begin{bgsolution}
Since \(f(0)=0\), the quotient is \(|h|/h\). For \(h>0\), \(|h|=h\), so the quotient is \(1\). For \(h<0\), \(|h|=-h\), so the quotient is \(-1\). Thus
\[
f'_+(0)=1,\qquad f'_-(0)=-1.
\]
The one-sided derivatives disagree, so \(f'(0)\) does not exist.
\end{bgsolution}
\end{bgguided}

\begin{bgcheck}{differentiability-01}{multiple_choice}{continuous-not-differentiable}[Quick check]
Which statement best describes \(f(x)=|x-3|\) at \(x=3\)?
\begin{enumerate}[label=\Alph*.]
\item discontinuous;
\item continuous and differentiable;
\item continuous but not differentiable;
\item differentiable but not continuous.
\end{enumerate}
\begin{bgreveal}The graph has a corner at \(x=3\), so it is \(\boxed{\text{continuous but not differentiable}}\).\end{bgreveal}
\end{bgcheck}


\begin{bgadvanced}[title={Why differentiability forces continuity}]
If \(f'(a)\) exists, then
\[
f(x)-f(a)=\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a}(x-a).
\]
As \(x\to a\), the quotient approaches \(f'(a)\) and the factor \(x-a\) approaches \(0\), so their product approaches \(0\). Hence \(f(x)\to f(a)\). The proof is short because the derivative already contains exactly the difference needed to prove continuity.
\end{bgadvanced}
