\section{Follow the Dependency, Not the Typography}
\lessonobjective{Identify outer and inner functions in several notational forms and predict the chain-rule factors before calculating.}

\begin{bgapplication}[title={Rates multiply because each stage converts units}]
If pressure changes voltage at $0.5$ volts per kilopascal and voltage changes a reading at $8$ display units per volt, then pressure changes the reading at $(8)(0.5)=4$ display units per kilopascal. The intermediate volts cancel in the units just as $du$ cancels in $\frac{dy}{du}\frac{du}{dx}$.
\end{bgapplication}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={Before the formulas}]
Composition is the hidden architecture of \emph{A Visual and Verbal Map of the Chain Rule}. An expression may look like one formula while actually containing several function machines. Draw or list the stages before differentiating. This is especially important when product, quotient, and chain rules appear together, because the outer algebraic connection and the inner compositions must both be respected.

Do not simplify away useful structure too early. A factored derivative often shows each chain factor more clearly and is easier to verify. Simplify after the calculus unless rewriting first genuinely reduces the number of rules required.
\end{bgexposition}



\begin{bgexposition}[title={Name the layers before touching the derivative}]
A nested formula becomes manageable when you label its layers. In \((1+\sin(3x))^5\), the outer operation is ``raise to the fifth power,'' inside that is ``add one,'' inside that is sine, and inside sine is multiplication by three.

Differentiation walks back through those layers from outside to inside. Each layer contributes one derivative factor. Writing the layer map first prevents missing an inner factor, the most common chain-rule error.
\end{bgexposition}

A composite function is a dependency chain. If \(u=g(x)\) and \(y=f(u)\), then changing \(x\) changes \(u\), and changing \(u\) changes \(y\). The total response is the product of those two local responses:
\[
\frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{dy}{du}\frac{du}{dx}.
\]

The same structure may be written as \(f(g(x))\), \((3x^2+1)^5\), \(\sin(x^3)\), or \(e^{\sqrt{x}}\). Typography changes; dependency does not.

\begin{bgtranslation}[title={Say the layers aloud}]
For \(y=\cos((x^2+1)^3)\), the layers are: cosine of a cube of a quadratic-plus-one. The derivative therefore needs three factors: derivative of cosine, derivative of the cube, and derivative of the quadratic-plus-one.
\end{bgtranslation}

\begin{bgcheck}{chain-map-01}{integer}{3}[Quick check]
How many nonconstant chain-rule layers appear in \(e^{\sin(x^2)}\)?
\begin{bgreveal}
There are \(\boxed{3}\): exponential, sine, and square. The derivative is \(e^{\sin(x^2)}\cos(x^2)2x\).
\end{bgreveal}
\end{bgcheck}
