\chapter{Advanced Notes: A Preview Beyond Calculus I}
\section{Why Include These Notes?}
These pages are optional. They do not add new differentiation techniques to memorize. They show that the familiar rules are shadows of broader ideas: local linear approximation, intermediate-value behavior, composition of linear maps, and local solvability of equations. A first-time learner may read them for perspective and continue without completing the exercises.
\webnext{subjects/math/calculus/derivatives/advanced-local-linearity-little-o}{Differentiability and Little-o Notation}

\section{Errors That Repeat Until They Are Named}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Evaluating \(f(a+h)\) incompletely.} Replace every occurrence of the input by the full expression \(a+h\).
\item \textbf{Cancelling terms instead of factors.} Factor first; cancellation applies to common multiplicative factors.
\item \textbf{Forgetting the chain factor.} Audit every inner function after differentiating.
\item \textbf{Using \((fg)'=f'g'\).} The product rule has two terms.
\item \textbf{Reversing the quotient-rule numerator.} Use \(f'g-fg'\) and keep the order consistent.
\item \textbf{Dropping \(y'\) in implicit differentiation.} Every differentiated \(y\)-expression needs the chain rule.
\item \textbf{Confusing inverse and reciprocal.} \(f^{-1}\) is the inverse function; \(1/f\) is the reciprocal.
\item \textbf{Using the power rule on \(x^x\).} The exponent is not constant; take logarithms.
\item \textbf{Ignoring domains.} Logarithms, roots, denominators, and inverse trig derivatives carry restrictions.
\item \textbf{Expanding a useful factored result into debris.} Simplification should reveal structure, not punish it.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{bgmethod}[title={Repair protocol}]
For every missed problem, write:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the structure you should have recognized;
\item the rule that applies;
\item the first incorrect line;
\item one nearby problem solved correctly without notes.
\end{enumerate}
\end{bgmethod}
